    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151559199": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Product Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Product Manager - Enterprise AI | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Product Manager - Enterprise AI Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60015409 UoA Department Name: Experience Innovation Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Product Manager - Enterprise AI to lead the University's vision, strategy and roadmap for Artificial Intelligence. This role will play a critical part in shaping how AI is adopted and applied across the University, ensuring investments deliver measurable value while aligning with the University's AI Action Plan, governance frameworks and digital strategy. Working across business domains, digital delivery teams, data specialists, architecture, security and senior stakeholders, you will identify opportunities where AI can improve services, decision-making and operational efficiency. You will take ownership of the AI product lifecycle, from discovery and business case development through to implementation, governance, measurement and continuous improvement. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced Product Manager to help define the future of AI at the University. You will be responsible for balancing innovation with responsible delivery, ensuring AI initiatives are supported by strong data governance, appropriate security controls, clear outcomes and robust return on investment. While you will not develop AI models directly, you will work closely with technical teams to translate business challenges into AI-enabled solutions that are practical, scalable and trustworthy. Your responsibilities will include: Developing and maintaining the University's AI vision, roadmap and product strategy Leading the prioritisation and lifecycle management of AI-enabled products and services Partnering with architecture, security, data and delivery teams to ensure AI solutions are scalable, secure and governed appropriately Building business cases and evaluating opportunities based on value, cost, risk and measurable outcomes Engaging and influencing stakeholders across the University to drive adoption and understanding of AI initiatives Promoting best practice in responsible AI, data governance and ethical decision-making This is a 24-month fixed-term, full-time position (37.5 hours per week). The salary range for this role is $135,000 to $155,000 , negotiable depending on skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked HERE He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced Product Manager who enjoys working at the intersection of strategy, technology and business outcomes. You have a strong understanding of AI and machine learning concepts and can confidently assess opportunities, risks and value propositions associated with AI-enabled solutions. You are comfortable leading conversations around governance, security, architecture, data quality and return on investment, and can translate complex technical concepts into clear business outcomes. You bring a collaborative approach, strong stakeholder management skills, and experience guiding products through their full lifecycle, from discovery and prioritisation through to delivery and continuous improvement. You are equally comfortable working with senior leaders, business owners, architects, security teams and technical specialists, helping align diverse perspectives around a shared vision and measurable outcomes. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking Significant experience in Product Management, Product Ownership or a similar strategic leadership role within a large and complex organisation Strong understanding of AI and machine learning concepts, including governance, risk, security and responsible AI practices Experience developing product roadmaps, business cases and investment priorities that deliver measurable outcomes and return on investment Demonstrated ability to lead cross-functional initiatives and build alignment across business, data, architecture, security and delivery teams Strong stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to influence senior leaders and translate technical concepts into business value Experience managing the full product lifecycle, including discovery, prioritisation, delivery, governance and continuous improvement Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday, 11 October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant, neil.gardner@auckland.ac.nz Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist Auckland, New Zealand Functional Analyst Manager Auckland, New Zealand Development Manager - Fundraising Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Product Manager - Enterprise AI Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151525599": {
      "title": "Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist",
      "description": "University of Auckland Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019814 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Job Description Join a newly established team dedicated to deepening customer relationships and driving long-term commercial growth for UOA. Enjoy hybrid work, a 37.5-hour week, and inclusive benefits designed to support wellbeing and caring commitments With more than 47,000 students and over 6,000 staff, Waipapa Taumata Rau, the University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand's leading university and a globally connected organisation dedicated to creating knowledge, transforming lives and shaping the future. As part of a newly established Marketing & Recruitment operating model, our Business Partnering team sits at the centre of strategic decision-making, helping faculties, research institutes and service divisions connect organisational priorities with meaningful marketing and recruitment outcomes. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist (known internally as Digital and Content Specialist) is responsible for the development and delivery of personalised, omnichannel customer lifecycle programs that drive meaningful business results. This is a great opportunity for someone who thrives where creativity, data, and martech intersect. As a key member of the Digital & Performance team, you will work closely with Marketing, Recruitment, Insight and Operations to translate business priorities into clear, actionable plans that directly impact the bottom line. About the role: Act as the Subject Matter Expert (SME) for Customer Lifecycle Management within Marketing and Recruitment, elevating stakeholder understanding and demonstrating clear bottom-line impact. Take end-to-end ownership of lifecycle activities within your assigned portfolio—developing, managing, and continuously optimizing campaigns to align with overarching strategy. Act as the guardian of the CLM strategic frameworks, ensuring compliance across all campaign executions. Collaborate with external agencies and internal teams (digital, marketing, and operations) to deliver cohesive, data-driven, and frictionless customer experiences. Build scalable campaign assets, including landing pages, reusable templates, and content frameworks, to support efficient delivery. Monitor and report on performance, extracting actionable insights that directly drive business OKRs. Maintain strict compliance with local and global privacy and data governance regulations, embedding a privacy-first approach across all touchpoints. This is a full-time, permanent opportunity. The full salary range for this role is $87,000 - $118,000 (Band [G]). We have a structured salary framework with three zones for those developing into the role (developing), fully effective (target) and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $87,000 – $107,000 , depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you You’re a marketing professional with a minimum of 5 years’ experience developing and delivering effective omnichannel CLM campaigns, leveraging multiple martech platforms. You’re passionate about and have a sound understanding of CLM as a core discipline to delivering hyper-personalisation at scale, and can translate campaign reports into actionable insights and plans. You’re also poised under pressure, highly organised and enjoy solving problems in a fast-paced multistakeholder environment. Whether you’re supporting your manager in developing a new strategy, optimising workflows, or analysing performance, you take a detail-oriented, privacy-first, and customer-focused approach. You enjoy working collaboratively and embrace digital tools and new ways of working to achieve business objectives. What you’ll bring to the role: Sound CLM & CRO Knowledge: An intuitive grasp of customer lifecycle management and CRO principles, with demonstrated experience applying them successfully in complex environments. Hands-on Campaign Execution: Proven capability to design, build, test, and optimise scalable automated campaigns, workflows, and digital touchpoints. Martech Proficiency: Hands-on experience with CRM, CDP, and marketing automation platforms (such as Salesforce Marketing Cloud, Eloqua, or similar enterprise tools). Data & Insights Focus: A results-driven, curious mindset with strong analytical skills to dig into performance data, uncover insights, and drive continuous optimisation. Technical Problem-Solving: The curiosity and technical capability to explore new platform capabilities, troubleshoot blockers, and improve system efficiency. Stakeholder & Influence Skills: Strong communication, presenting and interpersonal skills to manage multiple priorities, collaborate across teams, and advocate for strategy effectively. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer This is a rare opportunity to contribute to a new function, vision and culture while working within an organisation that operates at a significant scale. This new function gives you access to internal expertise and resources commensurate with our complexity and commitments, including an in-house design studio and media production teams, audience specialist teams, web and digital teams, AI and automation expertise, and Māori and Pacific specialists across brand, media, comms and engagement. As a comprehensive university, our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. Whether your interests lie in education, research, innovation, reputation building, community engagement or student growth, you'll have the chance to contribute across a wide range of portfolios, projects and strategic priorities while continuing to grow your own career. Beyond the work itself, you'll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5 hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns, as well as discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks' annual leave and additional leave buy/sell scheme Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including Kōhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks’ full pay, on-site parenting and feeding spaces A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongoā services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, discounted health insurance The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo Māori learning programmes for staff The opportunity to build your career with one of Aotearoa’s most diverse and complex organisations Find out more about the ways we are transforming how we engage with our internal and external audiences across our Marketing, Recruitment, Communications and Engagement functions. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of Friday, 16 October 2026 . Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities and relevant experience. Please reach out to Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant, at [email protected] with any questions. Reasonable accommodations - We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Whānau and support people - Should you be invited to an interview for this role, your whānau members and/or support people will be welcome to join you. The recruiter or hiring manager will provide more information about this as part of the interview arrangements. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151521319": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Economics | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Economics Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55565088 UoA Department Name: Economics Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. With more than 7,500 students, the Triple Crown accredited University of Auckland Business School is one of the largest and most successful in the Australasian region. The University of Auckland's position in the top 100 of the 2026 QS World Universities Ranking (65th globally, 1st in New Zealand) confirms both its status as New Zealand's pre-eminent research-led university and its place amongst the top 1% of universities worldwide. The University of Auckland Business School has an unconditional commitment to quality teaching and research in topics that are forward-focused and relevant to the commercial world today. The Department offers comprehensive undergraduate, postgraduate, and PhD programmes. Faculty members are active in publishing in leading international peer-reviewed journals, attracting external grants, supervising doctoral students, and maintaining links with the profession, industry, and government. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking Lecturers who demonstrate strong research potential, as well as a commitment to excellent research-informed teaching that is enhanced by appropriate learning technologies. Continuation (tenure) is based on performance in research, teaching, and service. In addition to growing your academic profile and reputation, you will also be committed to shaping and delivering an exceptional learning experience for students, contributing to an innovative teaching environment using a variety of methods. We invite applications from candidates in any field but are particularly interested in applicants whose work explores areas including Econometrics (Theoretical and Applied) and International Trade. He kōrero mōu | About you The successful appointee will have a PhD (or be near completion), with demonstrated excellence in research and a commitment to high quality research-informed teaching. You will have a research program that complements and builds upon existing areas of strength within the Department. You will be a highly effective teacher of both undergraduate and graduate students. The appointee will be expected to teach at undergraduate and postgraduate levels in their specialist area, at introductory levels more widely, and to engage in research and publication both personally and through the supervision of research students. You will also be expected to seek research funding, to engage with the profession and academic and non-academic stakeholders, and to contribute to departmental service. Flexibility in teaching and research collaboration within and beyond the Department are essential. All applications should provide the selection committee with: A cover letter and curriculum vitae Any teaching evaluations Job market paper This is a full time, 40 hours per week, permanent employment contract . The salary ranges are (per annum salary depending on experience and qualifications): The full remuneration range is: Lecture step 1: $99,788 – Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience Senior Lecturer - $126,800 - $158,900 NZD, depending on qualifications and experience For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review this position description. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave Research and study leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information please visit Staff Benefits . Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply All applications must provide the selection committee with: A cover letter and curriculum vitae Any teaching evaluations Full publication record Job market paper Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 26th October 2026 (NZ time) to be considered. Please highlight how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Prasanna Gai - Head of Department Economics via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151762609": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Innovation Programmes Adviser",
      "description": "University of Auckland Innovation Programmes Adviser - Māori Engagement | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Innovation Programmes Adviser - Māori Engagement Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55561997 UoA Department Name: Innovation & Entrepreneurship Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s pre-eminent University, with a turnover of $1.6bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. With over 9,000 staff and 50,000 students, the University of Auckland is one of New Zealand’s most significant and complex entities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Ko te pae tawhiti, whāia kia tata; ko te pae tata, whakamaua kia tīna. As an Innovation Programmes Adviser, you’ll work alongside other members of the Innovation Programmes Team to plan, coordinate and deliver programmes and events across CIE’s wider innovation and entrepreneurship portfolio. In addition to this core role, you’ll have particular responsibility for selected Māori-focused programmes and cohorts, including their delivery, participant experience, outreach and associated relationships. Plan, coordinate, deliver and continuously improve assigned innovation and entrepreneurship programmes and events across CIE’s portfolio. Manage the practical elements of programme delivery, including participants, contributors, communications, schedules, logistics, resources and post-programme reporting. Create high-quality participant experiences and help participants connect and progress through CIE’s wider opportunities and pathways. Work with academics, facilitators, practitioners and other contributors to deliver engaging and effective programmes. Take particular responsibility for selected Māori-focused programmes, cohorts and activities. Support participant recruitment, outreach and relationship-building for these Māori-focused programmes and cohorts, as well as Māori participation across CIE’s wider portfolio of programmes and opportunities. Bring the cultural capability and understanding of tikanga Māori needed to deliver Māori-focused programmes effectively and appropriately, and to support Māori participants across CIE’s wider portfolio. Work collaboratively with other Programme Advisers and the wider CIE team to deliver a connected programme portfolio. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent role. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description . Mō tō mātou rōpū | About the team The Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship (CIE) is Waipapa Taumata Rau’s platform for developing the innovators, entrepreneurs and changemakers of the future. We work across the University to help students, researchers and staff build innovation and entrepreneurial capability, turn ideas into action, develop ventures, and create social, cultural, environmental and economic impact for Aotearoa and the world. CIE is entering an exciting new chapter. With an ambitious new strategy, we are significantly increasing the reach and impact of innovation and entrepreneurship across the University, helping realise Waipapa Taumata Rau’s ambition to be a global powerhouse of innovation, creativity and entrepreneurship, and strengthening the University’s contribution to a more innovative and prosperous Aotearoa. We are building a team of people who are curious, collaborative, ambitious for impact and committed to turning ambition into action. If you want to help shape how a world-class research-led university develops innovative and entrepreneurial people, we would love to hear from you. He kōrero mōu | About you You are passionate about creating opportunities for others and have strong programme and event delivery skills. You’re highly organised, proactive and comfortable taking responsibility for programmes from planning through to delivery and review, coordinating multiple contributors and creating high-quality experiences for participants. You will bring experience working with Māori participants, communities or cohorts, and the cultural capability to plan and deliver programmes in ways that are relevant, welcoming and grounded in tikanga Māori. You’ll be confident bringing people together, building connection within cohorts, and working with Māori contributors and partners to create programmes that support participation and progression. Experience planning  and delivering programmes and events in education, innovation, entrepreneurship, community development or a related field. Strong programme management and organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple activities, contributors and priorities. Demonstrated experience engaging with Māori communities and an understanding of tikanga Māori and culturally responsive practice. Ability to engage effectively with Pacific communities and support participation and relationship-building initiatives. Strong relationship-building, communication and participant engagement skills. Experience working with facilitators, educators, practitioners, academics or community partners to deliver successful programmes. Initiative and sound judgement, with a practical approach to getting things done in a busy and varied environment. Ability to gather participant feedback and report of programme outcomes. A genuine interest in innovation and entrepreneurship and in making opportunities accessible and relevant to diverse participants. A relevant qualification and capability in te reo Māori, or a demonstrated commitment to developing proficiency. What we offer | Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary ($78,000 - $90,000pa) reflecting experience with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 11th October, 2026 to be considered. Please include your CV and a cover letter highlighting how you meet the skills and experience outlined above, what attracts you to this opportunity, and what you would bring to CIE and our work with Māori and Pacific communities. If you would value an informal kōrero about the role, the team or the kaupapa before applying, please contact Josh Chapman j.chapman@auckland.ac.nz Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Editor – Digital Content Auckland, New Zealand Student Support Adviser (Māori) Auckland, New Zealand Digital & Lifecycle Content Specialist Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Innovation Programmes Adviser - Māori Engagement Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151759720": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "University of Auckland Postdoctoral Fellow - Professor in MRI | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Postdoctoral Fellow - Professor in MRI Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022308 UoA Department Name: Liggins Institute Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We have an opportunity for an energetic, enthusiastic academic with experience in brain MRI to join a clinical research group at the Liggins Institute, University of Auckland.  The successful candidate will be involved in overseeing longitudinal analysis of MRI studies in neonates and children, including MoPED and STAMP. MoPED is a cohort study in moderate-late preterm babies investigating the relationships between early brain imaging findings and later development. STAMP is a school-age follow-up study of MoPED plus another moderate-late preterm cohort, including brain MRI and developmental assessment. The appointee will have day-to-day responsibility for ensuring appropriate collection, storage, processing and analysis of diffusion, structural and functional MRI data in these study cohorts. Analyses will include assessing relationships between neonatal clinical variables, developmental outcomes, and brain MRI findings in the neonatal period and at school age. This fulltime three-year post will provide an opportunity to work closely with a multidisciplinary team of clinicians, developmental and MRI researchers and statisticians at the Liggins Institute, Auckland, and collaborate with researchers at the University of British Columbia and the University of Toronto.   It will be an exciting opportunity for an applicant with a background in brain MRI processing and analysis, ideally in infants and children. Applicants from postdoc to professor level will be considered. Salary will be on the University of Auckland salary scales depending on experience. This range is $92,213 (postdoctoral fellow) - $190,294 (professor) For more detailed information please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You will be a collaborative and motivated researcher who enjoys working across disciplines to improve understanding of infant and child development. You will bring a strong background in MRI processing and analysis, excellent communication skills, and the ability to work both independently and as part of a highly regarded research team. Success in this role will come from your analytical capability, attention to detail, and enthusiasm for research that has real-world impact on maternal, fetal and child health outcomes. You will be comfortable working in a dynamic academic environment, building strong relationships with colleagues and collaborators while contributing to a culture of excellence and innovation. You will bring: PhD or equivalent qualification. Experience with MRI image processing and analysis packages. Expertise in diffusion, structural and/or functional MRI methodologies. Strong writing, reporting and presentation skills. Experience with scripting or programming for image processing workflows. Ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary research teams. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home if feasible, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 16 October 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to distinguished Professor Jane Harding, via [email protected] , for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151722379": {
      "title": "University of Auckland General Practitioner",
      "description": "University of Auckland General Practitioner | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . General Practitioner Part-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55556474 UoA Department Name: Student Health and Counselling Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are currently seeking an experienced General Practitioners to join our supportive and collaborative team. In this role, you will provide high-quality, patient-centred primary health care to a diverse student population. Working within a multidisciplinary team of doctors, nurses, counsellors, and psychologists, you will support students’ physical and mental wellbeing, helping them navigate the unique challenges of university life. This is a rewarding opportunity to work in a wellbeing-focused environment where preventative care, early intervention, and holistic support are at the heart of the service we provide. Your work will make a meaningful difference to students at a pivotal stage of their lives. Please note that we offer 20 minute appointments to our patients. Key responsibilities include: Providing skilled health assessment, diagnosis, and treatment services in line with current best practice and professional standards Delivering patient-centred care that supports both physical and mental wellbeing Working collaboratively with nurses, counsellors, and other health professionals to provide integrated care Managing referrals, documentation, follow-up care, and case management in a timely and professional manner Supporting preventative health initiatives, including screening programmes, vaccinations, and health education Providing travel health advice and vaccinations where required Assessing aegrotat applications and providing appropriate academic health advocacy Building strong relationships with internal and external healthcare providers and referral agencies These are permanent positions, and we are flexible with hours — we welcome applications from both full-time and part-time practitioners, depending on your availability. For more information, please review the Position Description. He kōrero mōu | About you You are a compassionate and experienced clinician who enjoys working with young adults and supporting diverse communities. You bring strong clinical judgement, excellent communication skills, and a calm, professional approach to patient care. You will bring: Registration as a medical practitioner in New Zealand with a current NZMC Practising Certificate and indemnity insurance Fellowship of the Royal New Zealand College of General Practitioners. At least two years’ experience in General Practice, ideally including primary or emergency care Current CPR or Advanced Life Support certification Experience working in a multidisciplinary healthcare environment Proven experience working with young adults and diverse communities Confidence using clinical systems such as MedTech or similar practice management software Experience working within a university or student-focused health service would be an advantage but is not essential. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . How to apply | Me pēhea te tuku tono Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 23/10/2026 to be considered. For any job-specific questions, please reach out to Tania Abernethy-Jones via tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz . Please include your cover letter and/or CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Innovation Programmes Adviser - Māori Engagement Auckland, New Zealand Postdoctoral Fellow - Professor in MRI Auckland, New Zealand Medical Lead Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. General Practitioner Auckland, New Zealand Part-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151722149": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Medical Lead",
      "description": "University of Auckland Medical Lead | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Medical Lead Part-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021163 Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Within Te Kāhu Tauira | Student & Scholarly Services, the University Health and Counselling Service (UHCS) provides high-quality, accessible healthcare and wellbeing support to students across the University. Our multidisciplinary team is committed to delivering exceptional care, promoting wellbeing, and helping students thrive throughout their university journey. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an experienced and inspiring Medical Lead to provide clinical leadership and strategic direction within our University Health and Counselling Service. This is a unique opportunity to combine clinical excellence with leadership in a purpose-driven environment that places student wellbeing at the heart of everything we do. As Medical Lead, you will play a key role in shaping the delivery of primary healthcare services for a diverse student population while supporting a team of dedicated clinicians to provide outstanding patient-centred care. Working closely with the General Manager and senior leadership team, you will contribute to service planning, clinical governance, quality improvement initiatives, and the ongoing development of a positive, collaborative, and high-performing clinical culture. This role offers the opportunity to make a meaningful impact on student health outcomes while helping shape the future of healthcare delivery within one of New Zealand’s leading universities. Key responsibilities include: Providing clinical leadership and support to medical practitioners within UHCS Promoting excellence in clinical practice, patient safety, and quality improvement initiatives Supporting clinical governance activities and ensuring compliance with relevant professional standards and legislation Contributing to service planning, operational decision-making, and strategic initiatives Working collaboratively with multidisciplinary teams including nurses, counsellors, psychologists, and administrative staff Supporting workforce development, mentoring, and professional growth within the medical team Maintaining a high standard of clinical care through direct patient consultations Supporting the development and implementation of evidence-based healthcare practices Building strong relationships with internal stakeholders and external healthcare providers Contributing to a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and student-centred service delivery This role combines clinical leadership with hands-on practice, allowing you to continue delivering care while helping drive service excellence and positive health outcomes for students. This is a full-time , 37.5 hours per week role on a permanent contract. For more information, please review the Position Description . He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced and respected medical practitioner who is passionate about delivering high-quality healthcare and supporting the development of others. You bring strong clinical leadership skills, sound judgement, and the ability to foster positive relationships across multidisciplinary teams. You are committed to continuous improvement, patient-centred care, and creating an inclusive and supportive environment where both students and colleagues can thrive. Most importantly, you are motivated by the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to student wellbeing while helping shape the future direction of a trusted and highly valued health service. Ngā pūkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience Current registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand and a current practising certificate Significant experience working in General Practice or primary healthcare Demonstrated clinical leadership experience and the ability to support and mentor clinicians Strong understanding of clinical governance, quality improvement, and patient safety principles Excellent communication and relationship-building skills Experience working effectively within multidisciplinary healthcare teams A commitment to culturally responsive practice and equitable health outcomes The following would be beneficial: Vocational registration in General Practice Experience working with young adults or within tertiary health services Experience leading service improvement and healthcare innovation initiatives Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland provides a supportive, people-centred working environment where your wellbeing and professional development are genuinely valued. As part of our team, you’ll enjoy: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Five weeks’ annual leave A wide range of professional development opportunities Generous parental leave provisions Access to staff discounts, recreation facilities, wellbeing initiatives, and discounted parking The opportunity to combine clinical practice with leadership in a rewarding university healthcare environment The chance to make a lasting impact on the health, wellbeing, and success of thousands of students Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online to be considered. Please submit your application by 23 October 2026 . Please include your cover letter and CV outlining how your skills and experience align with this role. For a confidential conversation about the role, please contact Tania Abernethy-Jones via tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz. Please note that applications sent via email will not be accepted. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Postdoctoral Fellow - Professor in MRI Auckland, New Zealand General Practitioner Auckland, New Zealand Development Manager - Fundraising Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Medical Lead Auckland, New Zealand Part-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152036579": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Development Manager",
      "description": "University of Auckland Development Manager - Fundraising  | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Development Manager - Fundraising Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 00016813 UoA Department Name: Alumni Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Development Manager to fundraise and partner with our Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences (FMHS) and institutes, connecting world-leading research, teaching and health initiatives with individual donors, corporate partners and philanthropic organisations. Together, these organisations form a nationally significant health, research and innovation portfolio dedicated to improving health outcomes for New Zealand and beyond. Bringing together expertise across medicine, health sciences, engineering and technology, they support groundbreaking research, transformative education and collaborative partnerships that address some of society's most pressing health challenges. As part of the Alumni Relations and Development team, you will manage a portfolio of major gift prospects and supporters, building strong relationships and developing tailored engagement strategies that lead to significant philanthropic investment. Working closely with the Dean, senior academic leaders, researchers and colleagues across the University, you will help identify opportunities that inspire donors to support transformative research, student success and innovation. This is a highly relationship-focused fundraising role that combines strategy, donor engagement and major gift development. You will guide prospects through the full donor journey, from identification and cultivation through to solicitation and stewardship, building trusted relationships with high-net-worth individuals, trusts, foundations and corporate partners. Success in this role comes from curiosity, initiative, strong commercial acumen and the ability to connect donors with opportunities that create meaningful and lasting impact. Your responsibilities will include: Managing a portfolio of major gift prospects, trusts, foundations and corporate partners, developing strategies to secure significant philanthropic support Building strong relationships with donors, faculty leadership, researchers and University stakeholders to identify and advance funding opportunities Developing donor engagement plans, proposals and stewardship activities that foster long-term relationships and investment Working alongside the Dean and senior leaders to prioritise prospects and align fundraising activity with faculty priorities Supporting the development and implementation of faculty fundraising strategies, plans and performance measures Collaborating with colleagues across Alumni Relations and Development to deliver coordinated donor engagement and fundraising initiatives This is a permanent, full-time position ( 37.5 hours per week). The salary range for this role is $118,000 to $128,000 , negotiable depending on skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role is provided in the position description linked HERE He kōrero mōu | About you You are an experienced fundraiser or relationship manager with a track record of building meaningful, long-term relationships that lead to significant financial investment. You are comfortable engaging with high-net-worth individuals, philanthropic trusts, foundations and senior corporate stakeholders, and understand how to move relationships from initial engagement through to successful solicitation and stewardship. You are naturally curious and enjoy understanding people's motivations, interests and aspirations. You know how to develop trust, identify alignment between donor interests and organisational priorities, and create compelling opportunities for support. Equally comfortable working independently and collaboratively, you thrive in a relationship-driven environment where success comes from strategic engagement, persistence and excellent stakeholder management. E rapua ana mātou | We are seeking Demonstrated success securing major gifts, philanthropic investment or high-value commercial partnerships through relationship-based engagement Experience managing a portfolio of high-net-worth individuals, trusts, foundations, corporate partners or key strategic stakeholders Proven ability to identify, cultivate, solicit and steward supporters through a structured relationship management or moves-management process Exceptional relationship-building and influencing skills, with confidence engaging senior leaders, donors and external stakeholders Demonstrable commercial acumen, with experience developing proposals, delivering presentations and achieving revenue or fundraising targets A relevant tertiary qualification, or equivalent experience, combined with a genuine interest in supporting world-leading medical research and health education Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on‑site amenities Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday, 18 October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant, via neil.gardner@auckland.ac.nz Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management Auckland, New Zealand Group Services Administrator - Permanent and Fixed Term Auckland, New Zealand Health Economist Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Development Manager - Fundraising Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152539129": {
      "title": "Animal Technician",
      "description": "University of Auckland Animal Technician | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Animal Technician Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022314 UoA Department Name: Liggins Institute Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a compassionate and reliable Animal Technician to join the Liggins Institute on an 8-month fixed-term basis, supporting a research study focused on improving lifelong health through world-leading science. This hands-on role is ideal for someone who is passionate about animal welfare, enjoys working in a research environment, and takes pride in maintaining high standards of care and accuracy. Working closely with an experienced research team, you will play an important role in the daily care and welfare of research animals, assisting with study procedures and ensuring accurate record-keeping throughout the project. Full training will be provided for study-specific procedures. Key responsibilities: Provide high-quality daily care, feeding, weighing, and welfare monitoring of research animals. Maintain accurate records, databases, and study documentation. Assist with sample and tissue collection, blood sampling, and metabolic assessments. Prepare treatments, consumables, and study materials as required. Support behavioural, physiological, and metabolic testing activities. Maintain high standards of hygiene, animal husbandry, and facility compliance. Contribute to health and safety practices and participate in research team meetings. This is a fulltime, fixed term position until 30 June 2027 The salary range for these roles are $63,600 - $79,300 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are someone who cares about animal welfare and thrives in a collaborative environment. You bring a meticulous approach to your work, strong organisational skills and the ability to follow protocols with care and precision. You enjoy supporting research and are comfortable balancing practical animal care responsibilities with accurate data collection and administration. With experience gained through animal care, laboratory environments, tertiary study, or industry work, you'll be keen to contribute to meaningful research that has the potential to improve health outcomes for future generations. You will also bring: Experience caring for animals, ideally within a research, laboratory, agricultural, or veterinary environment. Knowledge and understanding of animal welfare principles and regulations. Strong attention to detail and commitment to maintaining accurate records. Competency using Microsoft Office applications, particularly Excel/Word/Powerpoint. Excellent communication skills and ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary team. Previous experience working with laboratory animals would be advantageous. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 14 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Steffi Freudlsperger via [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152290238": {
      "title": "Postdoctural Research Fellow",
      "description": "University of Auckland Postdoctural Research Fellow - School of Computer Science - Te Kura Mātai Rorohiko - 32 month fixed term  | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Postdoctural Research Fellow - School of Computer Science - Te Kura Mātai Rorohiko - 32 month fixed term Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60022330 UoA Department Name: Computer Science Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare Pūtaiao | The Faculty of Science at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tuākana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, Māori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to help develop new computational and mathematical approaches to understanding autonomous agency — how living and artificial systems maintain themselves, regulate their own viability, adapt, and develop forms of intrinsic and emergent agency. You’ll work closely with Dr Matthew Egbert and an international network of collaborators from the University of Rochester, Santa Fe Institute, University of British Columbia, Dartmouth College, and the Basque Foundation for Science. You’ll join an exciting, interdisciplinary research project bringing together philosophers, systems biologists, physicists, computer scientists and complex systems researchers. Your work will focus on developing and analysing models of operational closure, viability, semantic information and emergent individuality, with the goal of developing rigorous formal frameworks for understanding how agency can arise and be organised in living and artificial systems. We’re looking for someone who is comfortable building and analysing computational models, using approaches such as agent-based modelling, dynamical systems simulation, network models, evolutionary models, artificial chemistries or related methods. Experience studying autonomy or agency before is preferred but not required. What’s important is a strong interest in using computational or formal approaches to explore fundamental questions about life, mind, individuality, adaptation and the organisation of complex systems. This is an opportunity to contribute to ambitious, interdisciplinary research, collaborate with leading researchers internationally, and develop your research profile in a highly collaborative environment. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), 32-month fixed-term position. The remuneration is $99,788 (RF1) per annum For more detailed information on the role, accountabilities, and requirements, please review the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You’ll have a PhD completed or nearing completion in a relevant field such as computer science, artificial life, applied mathematics, theoretical biology, philosophy of biology, philosophy of mind, complex systems or a related discipline. You might bring experience in areas such as: agent-based modelling dynamical systems simulation network or evolutionary models artificial chemistries information-theoretic approaches or other computational approaches to complex systems. You’ll be comfortable working independently while also contributing to a collaborative international research team, communicating your findings to both academic and wider audiences, and ideally have experience working across disciplinary boundaries. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date, 13th October 2026 , to be considered. The application must include: Cover Letter and CV, including a list of publications and preprints, a summary of teaching and service contributions, and the names of people who can provide academic references, plus an indication of availability to start if successful. Please reach out to Matthew Egbert (Senior Lecturer in Computer Science) via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Qualifications #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152099717": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022264 UoA Department Name: Health Systems Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting more than 46,000 students, we are one of New Zealand’s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Population Health, within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, is seeking a Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Health Leadership and Health Management. This is an opportunity to contribute to the development of future health leaders while advancing research and scholarship that supports the evolving needs of the health sector in Aotearoa New Zealand. Working across both the Master of Health Leadership and Bachelor of Health Sciences programmes, you will join a collaborative academic environment committed to improving health outcomes, reducing inequities, and strengthening health systems. You will work alongside experienced colleagues and sector partners to shape high-quality teaching, research, and engagement activities, while contributing to the future direction of health leadership education and scholarship. Key responsibilities Lead and contribute to teaching within Health Leadership and Health Management programmes. Design and deliver contemporary, research-informed learning and assessment. Supervise postgraduate student research and support student success. Undertake a programme of high-quality research relevant to health leadership and management in Aotearoa New Zealand. Develop and maintain strong relationships across the health sector. Contribute to programme development and the strategic direction of the Master of Health Leadership. Participate in service and leadership activities across the School, Faculty, and wider University community. This opportunity is for a permanent role, part time for 32 hours per week. The salary range for each academic level are: Lecturer: $99,788 - $119,204 (fulltime equivalent) Senior Lecturer: $126,863 - $145,741 (fulltime equivalent) Your salary will be determined based on your experience, qualifications, and the level at which you are appointed. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You will be an engaged academic or experienced sector leader with a strong interest in developing the next generation of health leaders. You bring a collaborative approach, excellent communication skills, and a commitment to working across diverse communities and organisations. You will be motivated by opportunities to connect scholarship with real-world impact and will value inclusive, culturally responsive approaches to teaching, research, and engagement. A commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its significance within the health sector is essential. You will bring: PhD in a relevant discipline such as health management, health leadership, public health, health systems, health policy, management, or organisational studies. Experience in leadership and/or management within the health or social sector. Demonstrated research capability in health leadership or health management. Understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its relevance to health. Ability to build and maintain relationships across the healthcare sector. Excellent organisational, communication, and collaborative skills. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world’s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home if feasible, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 27 October 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Professor Monique Jonas, via [email protected] , for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152101339": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Group Services Administrator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Group Services Administrator - Permanent and Fixed Term | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Group Services Administrator - Permanent and Fixed Term Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 55567015 UoA Department Name: Academic Operations Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We have two exciting opportunities for highly organised administrators to join the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences at the University of Auckland, based on our Grafton Campus. Working closely with academic and professional staff, you'll play an important role in supporting teaching, learning and programme administration while helping deliver an exceptional experience for staff and students. Role One: BHSc Programme Transition - Fixed-term (3 years), Full-time Join a significant programme transformation as the University launches a redesigned Bachelor of Health Sciences (BHSc). In this role, you'll provide high-quality administrative support to help coordinate programme implementation and support the successful transition to the new curriculum. Role Two: Bachelor of Biomedical Science (BBiomedSc) - Permanent, Full-time Join the team supporting the new Bachelor of Biomedical Science (BBiomedSc). Working alongside academic colleagues, you'll help coordinate teaching and programme administration that prepares students for careers in research and innovation through an integrated curriculum connecting the science of disease with real-world diagnosis and treatment. Key responsibilities: Provide comprehensive teaching and course administration support to academic staff. Coordinate curriculum information, course outlines and timetabling requirements. Support academic staff and students with Learning Management Systems, including course setup, content management and grade submission processes. Assist with assessment and examination administration, including tests, aegrotat applications and examiner nominations. Support postgraduate administration processes and student-related activities. Facilitate Staff-Student Consultative Committees and support stakeholder engagement. Contribute to continuous improvement initiatives while delivering excellent customer service. The salary range for these roles are $56,400 - $67,000 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are a proactive and adaptable administrator who enjoys building positive working relationships and supporting others to succeed. You take pride in delivering accurate, responsive service and can confidently manage competing priorities in a busy environment. You'll be comfortable working both independently and as part of a collaborative team, with the ability to engage effectively with a diverse range of stakeholders including academic staff, professional staff and students. A positive attitude, strong organisational skills and a willingness to support colleagues are key to your success. You will bring: Essential NCEA Level 3 qualification or equivalent experience. Minimum 3-4 years' experience in an administrative role within a large and complex organisation. Experience in a fast-paced administrative or programme coordination role, preferably within a tertiary education or complex public sector environment. Advanced skills in Microsoft Office applications and strong technological literacy. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build and maintain strong stakeholder relationships. Strong planning, organisational and problem-solving skills, with the ability to provide sound advice and exercise good judgement. Experience supporting multiple stakeholders and managing competing priorities. A proactive, flexible approach and the ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Adaptability and resilience, with the ability to work through periods of organisational change and ambiguity. A strong customer service focus and commitment to continuous improvement. Preferred A tertiary qualification. At least two years' experience working within an educational institution. Experience using Learning Management Systems (LMS). Experience working with student management systems or database-driven software. Previous experience supporting teaching and course administration processes. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 12 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role, and indicating which role you are applying for. If you have any questions, please email Bruce Rattray via bruce.rattray@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management Auckland, New Zealand Senior Clinics Administrator Auckland, New Zealand Research Operations Coordinator Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Group Services Administrator - Permanent and Fixed Term Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152759298": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Schools and Community Engagement Adviser",
      "description": "University of Auckland Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019816 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Are you a confident communicator and relationship builder who knows how to engage ambitious young people? We’re looking for a Schools and Community Engagement Adviser to join our high-performing team and help strengthen the University’s connections with secondary schools, students, whānau, and school communities. This is more than an outreach role. You’ll be part of the University’s student recruitment journey, helping attract high-achieving students and positioning UoA as a university of choice. Our Schools and Community Engagement approach is grounded in Manaakitanga, Whanaungatanga, and Kaitiakitanga — caring for people, building lasting relationships, and taking responsibility for the communities we work with. What you’ll be doing You’ll take ownership of an assigned portfolio of schools and become a trusted first point of contact for students, teachers, careers advisers, and school communities. Your days will be varied. You could be: Building strong relationships with schools and careers advisers and understanding what matters to their students. Representing at school visits, information evenings, events, and other engagement opportunities. Delivering confident, engaging presentations to students, teachers, and school leaders. Supporting the attraction and engagement of high-achieving secondary students, including students considering Cambridge and IB pathways. Using application, enrolment and engagement data to identify trends, opportunities and areas of focus. Developing tailored resources, digital content and engagement activities for different student audiences. Helping organise and deliver major University events and initiatives — and being someone who is comfortable rolling up their sleeves to make them happen. Working across the University with faculties, communications and marketing, student services and other teams to create a connected prospective student experience. The University of Auckland Contributing to projects and improvements that make it easier and more engaging for students to explore their future at The University of Auckland . Providing accurate, timely and genuinely useful advice to students and their whānau as they navigate their options. The role combines relationship management, recruitment, presentations, events, project work, data and strategy **Ideal start date January 2027** This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $73,400 to $90,700 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He kōrero mōu | About you You’re someone who can build rapport quickly, but you’re also comfortable holding your own. You’re confident, personable and strategic enough to see the bigger picture. You’ll bring: Excellent interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills. Strong presentation and facilitation skills, with the confidence to engage a room full of young people. A natural ability to build and maintain genuine professional relationships. The judgement to understand different audiences and tailor your approach accordingly. Strong organisation skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities, events and projects. A practical, solutions-focused approach — you see something that could work better and look for ways to improve it. Confidence working with data, systems and digital tools, with the ability to pick up new technology quickly. An analytical mindset and the ability to turn information and trends into useful insights. A strong customer and stakeholder focus, while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries. The confidence to work strategically and contribute ideas, rather than simply waiting for instructions. Must hold a full, clean New Zealand drivers licence Experience working within the New Zealand secondary school sector and an understanding of the tertiary environment would be valuable. Experience working with academically focused schools or students following Cambridge or IB programmes would be an advantage. An understanding of te ao Māori and confidence engaging respectfully with Māori and Pacific communities would also be highly valued. A commitment to equity and inclusion is important to how we engage with young people and their whānau. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: · Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) · Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme · A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 23rd October 2026, to be considered. We may close this advert early if we have a large number of applications. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Isabella Mory (Scholars Outreach & Engagement Manager) via [email protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153043779": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Finance Business Partner",
      "description": "University of Auckland Finance Business Partner - Research | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Finance Business Partner - Research Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60022134 UoA Department Name: Finance Business Advisory Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity 12-Month Fixed-Term Opportunity This is a strategic Finance Business Partner role supporting the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research portfolio. Working closely with senior leaders, you will provide trusted financial advice, planning, and performance insights that support informed decision-making and help deliver the University's research and organisational objectives. You will play a key role in strengthening financial capability, driving continuous improvement, and ensuring financial sustainability across a complex and dynamic environment. Partner with senior academic and professional leaders to develop financial strategies, annual plans, budgets, and forecasts aligned to organisational priorities. Provide high-quality financial analysis, reporting, and decision support to enable effective planning, performance monitoring, and resource allocation. Act as a trusted adviser to leadership teams, translating financial information into practical insights and recommendations that support strategic outcomes. Lead and contribute to initiatives that improve financial performance, operational effectiveness, and organisational capability across the portfolio. Develop strong relationships across faculties, research institutes, service divisions, and the wider finance community to ensure a collaborative and consistent approach to business partnering. Support financial governance and compliance by ensuring appropriate controls, policies, and performance frameworks are in place and effectively applied. Provide leadership and mentoring to finance team members while contributing to a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and service excellence. This is a 12-month secondment/fixed-term, full-time role (37.5 hours per week, Monday to Friday). The salary range for this role is $118,000-$136,000 depending on skills and experience. For more information, please see Position Description He kōrero mōu | About You You are a commercially astute finance professional who enjoys building strong relationships and influencing positive outcomes. Comfortable working in a complex organisation, you bring a collaborative approach, strong analytical capability, and the confidence to engage with senior stakeholders on both strategic and operational matters. A tertiary qualification in accounting, commerce, or a related discipline, together with Chartered Accountant status (CA ANZ, CPA, or equivalent). Significant experience in finance business partnering, financial planning, budgeting, and performance management within a large and complex organisation. Demonstrated ability to build credibility with senior leaders and provide clear, evidence-based advice that supports sound decision-making. Strong relationship management, communication, influencing, and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work across diverse groups and priorities. Experience leading or contributing to change initiatives, continuous improvement programmes, and the development of innovative solutions to business challenges. Proven ability to balance strategic thinking with operational delivery, while managing competing priorities and delivering high-quality outcomes. A collaborative leadership style with experience mentoring, supporting, or developing team members to achieve shared goals. Strong digital literacy, including an interest in emerging technologies and the ability to apply sound judgement when evaluating and using new tools. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand’s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver, matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance, memberships, and on‑site amenities Additional Information Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 18th October, 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend Posted by Joshua Chapman share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Credit Controller Auckland, New Zealand Student Support Advisor Auckland, New Zealand Faculty Assistant/Receptionist Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Finance Business Partner - Research Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152773579": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "University of Auckland Research Technician | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Research Technician Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022230 UoA Department Name: Physiology Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand’s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ’s largest employers.  We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated individual with excellent technical, communication, organisational and research skills to join the Molecular Vision Research Cluster within the Department of Physiology at the University of Auckland. In this role, you will support postgraduate students with animal handling and contribute to the delivery of research objectives associated with a Marsden-funded project. You will work across a range of laboratory techniques, including lens dissection, sample preparation for immunohistochemistry, and Western blot analysis. The successful candidate will thrive in a multidisciplinary research environment and work closely with postgraduate students and other members of the Molecular Vision Research Cluster. Strong communication skills, meticulous record-keeping, and the ability to work collaboratively as part of a team are essential. This is an exciting opportunity for someone who demonstrates a high level of organisation, attention to detail, and a commitment to maintaining the highest standards of research practice while contributing to impactful vision research. Key responsibilities include Assist in the planning, setup and delivery of experiments associated with a Marsden-funded research project. Support data collection and analysis activities. Assist with animal handling in accordance with approved research protocols. Support and train postgraduate students in relevant laboratory techniques. Undertake laboratory procedures including lens dissection, immunohistochemistry sample preparation and Western blot analysis. Maintain accurate and detailed laboratory records and research documentation. Work collaboratively with postgraduate students and researchers across the Molecular Vision Research Cluster. This is a part time (18.75h per week), fixed term position until 28/02/2029 , in accordance with grant funding. The salary range for this role is $63,600 - $79,300 depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you You are an organised and motivated researcher who enjoys working in a collaborative scientific environment. You bring strong technical capability, excellent communication skills, and a genuine interest in supporting both research outcomes and postgraduate student development. You are comfortable managing your own workload, working independently when required, and contributing positively as part of a multidisciplinary team. You take pride in maintaining high standards of research practice, have exceptional attention to detail, and understand the importance of accurate record-keeping in a research setting. You will also bring: A postgraduate qualification or relevant research experience; a Master's degree is preferred. Knowledge of biomedical research methodologies and laboratory techniques. Experience with, or willingness to undertake, animal handling. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Excellent written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills. A commitment to maintaining accurate records and high standards of research practice. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 14 October and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Julie Lim via [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153089099": {
      "title": "University of Auckland Student Support Advisor",
      "description": "University of Auckland Student Support Advisor | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Support Advisor Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022303 UoA Department Name: Student Support and Engagement Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Student Support and Engagement function delivers services that help students overcome personal barriers to success and to maximise their time at Waipapa Taumata Rau | University of Auckland. The purpose of the Student Support Adviser role is to work directly with students to identify and respond to their support needs in order to improve their experience and their likelihood of success at University. This position plays a critical role in supporting the University’s student retention and success objectives. Key Responsibilities: Identification of students at risk as well as high achieving students; creating, implementing, and reviewing support plans Establish learning support programmes and on-going operational support Monitoring progress of support plans Facilitate a strong, collaborative relationship for mentors and university-wide support programmes including faculty staff and clinical site managers and coordinators. Develop and maintain a close working relationship with central support functions in order to support students. Providing equity advocacy and advice. Demonstrate a high level of interpersonal skills through relationship management, advocacy and negotiation Work collaboratively toward continuous improvement both operationally and strategically Ensure actions support the delivery of the service promised and at all times demonstrate excellent customer service We have 3x fulltime positions available starting Monday 11 January 2027: Position 1 – permanent Position 2 – fixed term until December 2027 Position 3 – fixed term until December 2029 The salary range for this position is between $63,600 - $79,300 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you Our ideal candidate will be a confident problem solver and solution finder. You will be able to understand what a student needs and have the energy and focus to locate the right contact, department or guidance material that will help them do what they have set out to do. You will do this by being available for meetings in person and via video calls, and by being active and responsive to your emails and calls. We are searching for someone who is adaptable and confident as this role has a lot of variety but requires attention to detail and the ability to plan and execute quickly and effectively. Your strong interpersonal and communication skills, outstanding organisational abilities, and your friendly and engaging manner will be a key factor in your success in this role. Important skills: Experience in the delivery of academic and pastoral support for students across a busy and complex faculty. Experience working in an environment requiring high levels of customer service delivery. Experience and understanding of equity issues in New Zealand. Demonstrates problem solving ability requiring judgement and interpretation. Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 16 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. Please also advise which position you are applying for. If you have any questions, please email Andrew Williams via andrew.williams@auckland.ac.nz Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Faculty Assistant/Receptionist Auckland, New Zealand Media & Communications Partners Auckland, New Zealand Research Technician Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Student Support Advisor Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153088399": {
      "title": "Faculty Assistant/Receptionist",
      "description": "University of Auckland Faculty Assistant/Receptionist | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Faculty Assistant/Receptionist Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 00010565 UoA Department Name: Technical Services Research Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The Faculty of Medical and Health Science is seeking a proactive, service-oriented, and well-organised Faculty Assistant/Receptionist to work within and provide support to the Grafton 505 and 507 reception. You will be working alongside a team of dynamic people who have a collegial/calm, friendly work ethic with great personalities. The successful applicant will provide support for the Faculty main reception to ensure the smooth provision of general and staff administrative support such as (but not limited to), meeting and greeting visitors, handling front desk inquiries, facilities & support services, the management of car parking bookings and security incident reporting. You will report through to the Facilities and Services Manager and hold functional relationships with the Facilities and Services team, Group Services, Faculty Administration within the Dean’s office, Property Services and Marketing Events Coordinator - a group who have a wealth of excellent University knowledge. This is a part-time, permanent position with scheduled hours Monday - Friday 1pm - 6pm. Remote working is not available for this position. The salary range for this position is between $56,550 - $62,900 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you We are looking for a highly motivated and focused individual, who can deliver a high standard of service, and can also provide reliable, efficient, and accurate execution of the reception duties at our large campus This position will suit a confident, dynamic, and energetic individual with experience gained in administration or a reception related role. You will bring Excellent written and verbal communication skills displaying sensitivities to and understanding of student and staff needs. Ability to build relationships, problem solve and provide effective support will be critical Strong customer focus, excellent time management with strong prioritisation skills Ability to work as a team Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 16 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Amie Straker via [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153087609": {
      "title": "Stores Coordinator",
      "description": "University of Auckland Stores Coordinator | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Stores Coordinator Full-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 55560027 UoA Department Name: Technical Services Research Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa New Zealand’s leading university. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are proud to be one of the country’s largest and most diverse employers. Our people are driven by curiosity, purpose, and a shared commitment to creating positive impact for our communities. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a stores coordinator to join the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences Central Stores team. The Store Coordinator is responsible for procurement, inventory, and management of inwards goods activities on our Grafton Campus. This role is front facing with a high degree of customer service and communications. If you are an energetic and outgoing person who enjoys building relationships with external stakeholders, then this could be the job for you. Key Responsibilities: Inventory maintenance of all goods in the store/stockroom, including consumables and chemicals. Receiving inward goods, notifying recipients, and facilitating distribution to recipients. Manage the ongoing operational and management requirements for the assigned facility. Understand and comply with containment requirements for stock held within the Central Stores Facility Ensure safe storage, covered by these regulations, and ensure correct record keeping. Ensures that all Health and Safety operating procedures and regulations are adhered to This is a fulltime, permanent position with scheduled hours onsite. These hours will be between 7:00am to 4:00pm, Monday to Friday. Due to the nature of the role, a level of physical fitness is required, and remote working is not available for this position. The salary range for this position is between $56,550 - $62,900 depending on skills and experience For more detailed information, please refer to the position description He kōrero mōu | About you The ideal candidate will: Be reliable/dependable Have strong organisational skills Bring strong relationship building skills Be a clear communicator, both written and verbal Have prior experience working in a stores and stock control role Previous inwards/outward goods experience Have a current first aid qualification (or willing to obtain) Have a current forklift licence (or willing to obtain) Ngā āhuatanga kei a mātou | What we offer The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices and a culture Career development programmes A competitive salary with five weeks’ annual leave 6.75% superannuation scheme For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me pēhea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 16 October 2026 and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions, please email Amie Straker via [email protected] Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications via email #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.  For further information on services for Māori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakatāpu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share."
